  "worked_solution": "The function $q(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $q(3)=0\\,$  exists  $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)\\ne q(3)$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/d7fc2426919c72ff934d19e98eb7465b8a42852407809d242213da9c26a4ed4a.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=3,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=3$ but they are *not* removable.",
